  "headline_index": null,
  "what_the_study_did": "The study reviews animal models of kidney failure and discusses their contribution to clinical translation.",
  "what_it_found": "Some longstanding animal models have failed to result in therapeutic advances in the clinical setting, but recent novel models hold promise for clinical translation.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Some longstanding animal models have failed to result in therapeutic advances in the clinical setting, such as kidney ischemia-reperfusion injury and diabetic nephropathy models.",
      "topic": "Model Failures"
    },
    {
      "quote": "In this regard, most models for diabetic nephropathy are unsatisfactory in that they do not evolve to renal failure.",
      "topic": "Diabetic Nephropathy Models"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Satisfactory models for additional nephropathies are needed.",
      "topic": "Model Needs"
    },
    {
      "quote": "However, recent novel models hold promise for clinical translation.",
      "topic": "Promising Models"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Thus, the AKI to CKD translation has been modeled, in some cases with toxins of interest for human CKD such as aristolochic acid.",
      "topic": "AKI to CKD Translation"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Genetically modified mice provide models for Alport syndrome evolving to renal failure that have resulted in clinical recommendations, polycystic kidney disease models that have provided clues for the development of tolvaptan, that was recently approved for the human disease in Japan; and animal models also contributed to target C5 with eculizumab in hemolytic uremic syndrome.",
      "topic": "Successful Models"
    }
  ]
}
